ADS-017 — Interoperability Model

Document ID: ADS-017
Title: Interoperability Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Interoperability Model (CIM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Interoperability Model establishes the principles, architecture, and governance required for the reliable exchange, interpretation, and use of Canonical Atlas Objects across heterogeneous systems, organizations, domains, and implementation technologies while preserving engineering semantics.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Interoperability architecture
Interoperability levels
Exchange principles
Compatibility
Federation
Semantic preservation
Integration patterns
Governance

This specification applies to every exchange involving Canonical Atlas Objects.

3. Canonical Definition

Interoperability is the ability of independent systems to exchange, interpret, and use Canonical Atlas Objects while preserving semantic meaning, governance, provenance, and engineering intent.

Interoperability concerns meaning, not merely connectivity.

4. Objectives

The Interoperability Model SHALL:

Preserve engineering semantics.
Enable system independence.
Enable organizational federation.
Support technology diversity.
Support schema evolution.
Preserve governance.
Preserve traceability.
Enable long-term compatibility.
5. Interoperability Principles

Every interoperable Atlas implementation SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Semantic Preservation

Engineering meaning SHALL remain unchanged.

Canonical Exchange

Atlas objects SHALL be exchanged using canonical structures.

Technology Independence

Exchange SHALL remain independent of protocols and vendors.

Explicit Mapping

Semantic transformations SHALL be documented.

Traceability

Object provenance SHALL survive exchange.

Governance Preservation

Governance metadata SHALL accompany exchanged objects.

6. Interoperability Levels

Atlas defines five interoperability levels.

Level	Description
L1	Technical connectivity
L2	Structural interoperability
L3	Semantic interoperability
L4	Governance interoperability
L5	Cognitive interoperability

Only Level 5 ensures complete Atlas interoperability.

