8. Exchange Profiles

Atlas recognizes multiple exchange profiles.

Examples include:

Profile	Purpose
Internal	Between Atlas services
Enterprise	Between enterprise systems
Cross-Organization	Between organizations
Regulatory	Government exchange
Digital Twin	Digital engineering
AI	AI and ML interoperability

Profiles SHALL inherit the canonical interoperability principles.

9. Semantic Mapping

Semantic mappings SHALL define:

Source object
Target object
Mapping rules
Information loss (if any)
Validation rules
Transformation authority

Mappings SHALL be versioned.

10. Compatibility

Atlas defines four compatibility classes.

Compatibility	Description
Native	No transformation required
Adapted	Adapter performs mapping
Extended	Additional information supported
Partial	Some semantics unavailable

Compatibility SHALL be explicitly declared.

11. Federation

Atlas SHALL support federation across:

Organizations
Projects
Cloud providers
Geographic regions
Regulatory jurisdictions
Engineering disciplines

Federation SHALL preserve object identity and governance.

12. Integration Patterns

Supported patterns include:

Request–Response
Publish–Subscribe
Event Streaming
Batch Exchange
Digital Twin Synchronization
Workflow Coordination

Patterns SHALL remain independent of transport technologies.

13. Governance

Every interoperability profile SHALL define:

Exchange authority
Trust requirements
Security requirements
Validation requirements
Compliance requirements
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout the exchange.

14. Quality Requirements

Interoperable exchanges SHALL satisfy:

Semantic integrity
Structural integrity
Traceability
Completeness
Consistency
Compatibility
Security
Explainability

Quality SHALL be measurable.

15. Conformance

An interoperability implementation conforms if it:

Preserves canonical semantics.
Supports canonical Atlas objects.
Preserves governance metadata.
Preserves provenance.
Supports interoperability profiles.
Implements compatibility declarations.
Maintains traceability.
16. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Autonomous semantic negotiation.
Knowledge graph federation.
Cross-standard interoperability.
Digital engineering interoperability profiles.
AI-native exchange models.
International Atlas registries.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical interoperability semantics.

